Third-party assets: none are stored in this repository. Every asset added to it is listed with its
licence in ASSETS.md; licence texts live next to the assets.

Fonts compiled into the apps: the desktop and web apps embed egui's built-in fonts from the
`epaint_default_fonts` crate (https://github.com/emilk/egui), used for the user interface and, when
no system fonts are available (always in the browser), as fallback fonts for designs:
  * Ubuntu Light — Ubuntu Font Licence 1.0 (https://ubuntu.com/legal/font-licence)
  * Hack — MIT License, with the Bitstream Vera licence for the parts derived from it
    (https://github.com/source-foundry/Hack)
  * Noto Emoji — SIL Open Font License 1.1 (https://github.com/googlefonts/noto-emoji)
  * emoji-icon-font — SIL Open Font License 1.1 / MIT (https://github.com/jslegers/emoji-icon-font)
The licence texts ship with that crate (fonts/UFL.txt, fonts/OFL.txt, fonts/Hack-Regular.txt,
fonts/emoji-icon-font-mit-license.txt).

Video: the H.264 encoder and MP4 writer (crates/video) are original code written for GenHa from the
public specifications (Rec. ITU-T H.264, ISO/IEC 14496-12 and 14496-15); no third-party codec code is
included. H.264 is covered by patents licensed through a patent pool; this licence covers GenHa's
code only (see docs/architecture.md › Video).

Background removal models: none are included. On the user's request the apps download U²-Net /
U²-Netp (Xuebin Qin, Zichen Zhang, Chenyang Huang, Masood Dehghan, Osmar R. Zaiane and Martin
Jagersand, "U²-Net: Going Deeper with Nested U-Structure for Salient Object Detection", Pattern
Recognition 2020; Apache License 2.0, https://github.com/xuebinqin/U-2-Net) as ONNX files converted
by the rembg project (MIT, https://github.com/danielgatis/rembg). They run with tract (MIT OR
Apache-2.0, https://github.com/sonos/tract). Downloads use rustls (Apache-2.0 OR ISC OR MIT) and ring
(Apache-2.0 AND ISC, https://github.com/briansmith/ring).

The repository layout, layering and agent conventions follow the ArtCraft apps (VectorCraft,
MIT OR Apache-2.0, https://github.com/storytold/vectorcraft).
